{
  "requirement": "The `PlainText` implementation of `OutlineLang` supplies the extension, selection, and fingerprint rules described in this chapter.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub struct PlainText;\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for PlainText;\nimpl Debug for PlainText;\nimpl Default for PlainText;\nimpl OutlineLang for PlainText;\nimpl Copy for PlainText;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `PlainText` requirement has two parts. It says the type \"supplies the extension, selection, and fingerprint rules described in this chapter\". It also lists the type declaration and five trait impls: Clone, Debug, Default, OutlineLang and Copy. The tests cover both parts.\n\n**Extension rule** (claims no extensions, and is selected when no language claims the extension)\n- `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline]` asserts that `languages::PlainText` has `name()` equal to `\"plain-text\"` and an empty `extensions()`.\n- `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` builds `OutlineContent` from `missing.unclaimed`. That filename has an unclaimed extension, and a second case uses `missing`, which has no extension. Both get plain-text behaviour.\n\n**Selection rule** (a root plus one `line` item per nonblank line, with selectors `line@<12-hex-prefix>`)\n- `fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` checks the following:\n  - There are 4 items, and the root span is `0..18`.\n  - Blank lines create no item.\n  - Each item's kind is `line`, and its selector is `line@` plus 12 hex digits equal to the prefix of its structural hash.\n  - Identical lines share a selector, and different lines do not.\n  - `select` returns the right text.\n  - Reflowing the source keeps the selectors.\n  - The empty file yields only a root.\n\n**Fingerprint rule**\n- `fn[plain_line_fingerprints_use_sha256_words_without_losing_boundaries]` asserts these:\n  - A known SHA-256 value for the line `hello`.\n  - Whitespace inside a line does not change the hash.\n  - `two words` differs from `twowords`.\n  - The scheme is `plain-text-structural-v1`.\n- `fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` asserts these:\n  - Inserting blank lines or reflowing keeps the root fingerprint.\n  - Reordering or joining lines changes it.\n- `fn[outline_only_languages_report_unsupported_structural_search]` shows the plain-text structural-search behaviour.\n\n**Type declaration and trait impls**\n- The impls come from the `language!` macro in `src/outline/languages/mod.rs`. It uses `#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default)]` and one `impl OutlineLang`.\n- The derives are compiler-generated and contain no logic of their own.\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` is marked as a test of this requirement. Through `RustdocSignatures::trait_impls`, it compares the rustdoc-generated trait impl list against the spec's declared impls.\n- The `check` helper in `fn[bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline]` requires `OutlineLang + Default + Copy` for the other bundled languages. PlainText is not passed to it, but the API inventory test covers its trait impls.\n\n**Delegated obligations**\n- The detailed rules (`items`, `fingerprints`, `language`, `attachments`) are separate requirements. Each has its own tests, and the `attachments` test is `bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax`. They need no extra test under the subject.\n\nI found no requirement-violating behaviour that these tests would accept, so I report no gap.",
